[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Oracle Collections: Arrays, Nested Tables, and VARRAYs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Oracle collections are data structures that allow you to store and manipulate multiple values as a single unit. They are similar to arrays in other programming languages, but offer more flexibility and features. Oracle provides three main types of collections: Arrays (index-by tables), Nested Tables, and VARRAYs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Arrays (Index-by Tables)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Arrays, also known as index-by tables or associative arrays, are collections where elements are accessed using an index of a specific data type (usually VARCHAR2 or PLS_INTEGER). They are similar to hash tables or dictionaries in other languages."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"-- Declare an array indexed by VARCHAR2
DECLARE
  TYPE name_array IS TABLE OF VARCHAR2(50) INDEX BY VARCHAR2(30);
  names name_array;
BEGIN
  names('first') := 'John';
  names('last') := 'Doe';
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('First Name: ' || names('first'));
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Last Name: ' || names('last'));
END;
/"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, 'name_array' is a type definition for an array that stores VARCHAR2 values and is indexed by VARCHAR2 keys. The 'names' variable is then declared as an instance of this type.  Values are assigned and retrieved using the index."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Nested Tables"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Nested tables are collections that can store an arbitrary number of elements of the same data type. They are stored as a separate table in the database and can be accessed using SQL. Unlike VARRAYs, nested tables do not have a fixed size."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"-- Create a type for the nested table
CREATE TYPE phone_numbers_type AS TABLE OF VARCHAR2(20);
/

-- Create a table with a nested table column
CREATE TABLE employees (
  employee_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,
  employee_name VARCHAR2(50),
  phone_numbers phone_numbers_type
) NESTED TABLE phone_numbers STORE AS employee_phones;

-- Insert data into the table
INSERT INTO employees (employee_id, employee_name, phone_numbers)
VALUES (1, 'Alice Smith', phone_numbers_type('123-456-7890', '987-654-3210'));

-- Select data from the table
SELECT employee_id, employee_name, phone_numbers
FROM employees;
"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates creating a nested table type 'phone_numbers_type' and using it as a column in the 'employees' table. The 'NESTED TABLE' clause specifies that the 'phone_numbers' column is a nested table, and the 'STORE AS' clause specifies the name of the storage table for the nested table data."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"VARRAYs (Variable-Size Arrays)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"VARRAYs are collections with a fixed maximum size. They store elements of the same data type in a contiguous block of memory. Once the VARRAY is created, its maximum size cannot be changed. VARRAYs are suitable when you know the maximum number of elements in advance."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"-- Create a type for the VARRAY
CREATE TYPE address_array AS VARRAY(3) OF VARCHAR2(100);
/

-- Create a table with a VARRAY column
CREATE TABLE customers (
  customer_id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,
  customer_name VARCHAR2(50),
  addresses address_array
);

-- Insert data into the table
INSERT INTO customers (customer_id, customer_name, addresses)
VALUES (1, 'Bob Johnson', address_array('123 Main St', 'Apt 4B', 'Anytown'));

-- Select data from the table
SELECT customer_id, customer_name, addresses
FROM customers;
"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'address_array' is a VARRAY type that can store up to 3 VARCHAR2 values. The 'customers' table includes a column of this type. When inserting data, the VARRAY is initialized with the 'address_array' constructor."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Choosing the Right Collection Type"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arrays (Index-by Tables): Use when you need to access elements using a key (string or number) and the order of elements is not important."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Nested Tables: Use when you need to store a variable number of elements and access them using SQL. They are suitable for storing lists of related data."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"VARRAYs: Use when you know the maximum number of elements in advance and need to store them in a contiguous block of memory. They are suitable for storing small, fixed-size lists."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Collection Methods"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Oracle provides several built-in methods for working with collections. Some common methods include:"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"COUNT: Returns the number of elements in the collection."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"EXISTS(n): Returns TRUE if the nth element exists, FALSE otherwise."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"FIRST: Returns the index of the first element."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LAST: Returns the index of the last element."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"NEXT(n): Returns the index of the element after the nth element."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PRIOR(n): Returns the index of the element before the nth element."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"EXTEND: Adds one or more elements to the end of a nested table or VARRAY."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TRIM: Removes one or more elements from the end of a nested table or VARRAY."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"DECLARE
  TYPE num_array IS TABLE OF NUMBER;
  numbers num_array := num_array(10, 20, 30);
BEGIN
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Count: ' || numbers.COUNT);
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('First: ' || numbers.FIRST);
  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Last: ' || numbers.LAST);
END;
/"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates using the COUNT, FIRST, and LAST methods on a nested table."}]
